<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-Nepalese War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Nepalese–Tibetan War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-Nepalese War (1788–1792) began with Nepalese invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> over trade and coinage disputes, culminating in the sack of the Tashilhunpo monastery in 1791. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> sent a Qing army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukanggan">Fuk'anggan</a>, which drove the Nepalese out of Tibet and advanced to within reach of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> in 1792. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> accepted peace and sent tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> until the early twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-nepalese-war.jpg" alt="Sino-Nepalese War" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A collaboration between Chinese and European painters. The Jesuit missionaries involved in producing the drawings in ... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victory_banquet_at_the_Ziguangge_(Hall_of_Purple_Glaze).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
